{"title":"2015 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 23","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2015 AMC 12A Problems\">2015 AMC 12A #23</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems\">2015 AMC 10A #25</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Casework)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Casework)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Average_Function_Value/Quick_Faux_Integration)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Average Function Value/Quick Faux Integration)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Extension,_CALCULUS)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Extension, CALCULUS)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Area)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Area)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Richard_Rusczyk\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be a square of side length 1. Two points are chosen independently at random on the sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />. The probability that the straight-line distance between the points is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc960c165b21ba43f7a90caa2893d40409d6944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/6/c8648112d1e1c73002aa6d0b9420390b98a342ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a-b\\pi}{c}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"53\" height=\"37\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/b/f3b28547efd22be1c4a05fa193cd7038fc65d71b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"29\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> are positive integers and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25e152853b879e16286aca8f620e52bf396ee1ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{gcd}(a,b,c) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"112\" height=\"18\" />. What is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/b/6ebd1e0356cfe09c0d9b5459d6f1542657bb5891.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b+c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"72\" height=\"16\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/0/4708f47a030a142952cd61dc8b4f4feb03e47a68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 59 \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 60 \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 61 \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 62 \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 63$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"394\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Divide the boundary of the square into halves, thereby forming 8 segments. Without loss of generality, let the first point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be in the bottom-left segment. Then, it is easy to see that any point in the 5 segments not bordering the bottom-left segment will be distance at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/4/4a4a7df1100efe353cc612d5d1c289c22d5bf8c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> apart from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />. Now, consider choosing the second point on the bottom-right segment. The probability for it to be distance at least 0.5 apart from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/0/04091a7f1a77155363292d198457566338cf9fdd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{0 + 1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"80\" height=\"37\" /> because of linearity of the given probability. (Alternatively, one can set up a coordinate system and use geometric probability.)\n</p><p>If the second point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is on the left-bottom segment, then if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is distance <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> away from the left-bottom vertex, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> must be at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/7/75712a03a1a4b035c423a5b7cb5f96fe911883b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\sqrt{0.25 - x^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"123\" height=\"37\" /> away from that same vertex. Thus, using an averaging argument we find that the probability in this case is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf2454d6e2a5cc9b2f5fad7c6f0208f4d72a49.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2}^2} \\int_0^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\dfrac{1}{2} - \\sqrt{0.25 - x^2} dx = 4\\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{\\pi}{16}\\right) = 1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4}.\\]\" width=\"391\" height=\"52\" />\n</p><p>(Alternatively, one can equate the problem to finding all valid <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/f/adfd9ae8a3fac031d3b8b470a52a709a23d4d6d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/e/3def358a11bef0bd95c96661e0afb5bab9e29471.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 &lt; x, y &lt; \\dfrac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"97\" height=\"37\" />  such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/c/02cb585a3504574e5af941024cfac101334f4fb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + y^2 \\ge \\dfrac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"93\" height=\"37\" />, i.e. (x, y) is outside the unit circle with radius 0.5.)\n</p><p>Thus, averaging the probabilities gives\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/7/aa7365a0753af401afa0e0f926b6f02c513f49ea.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P = \\frac{1}{8} \\left(5 + \\frac{1}{2} + 1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{1}{32} (26 - \\pi).\\]\" width=\"308\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/6/a46f6af01fcf3bb5f6cac2316bc4de9bb3906bec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"31\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Casework.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Casework)\">Solution 2 (Casework)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Casework)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Fix one of the points on a SIDE. There are three cases: the other point is on the same side, an adjacent side, or the opposite side, with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/c/43c10c05747b98174223e6647cd8438f3e75f2fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.25, 0.5, 0.25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"103\" height=\"16\" />, respectively.\n</p><p>Opposite side: Probability is obviously <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, no matter what. \n</p><p>Same side: Pretend the points are on a line with coordinates <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/7/7171825a73418b7a616cd176328df3c8349ab4f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|a-b| \\le \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"85\" height=\"37\" />, drawing a graph will give probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Adjacent side: superimpose a coordinate system over the points; call them <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/9/eb99be0632ae9be9bbeeec99f75e4a71c2595180.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/8/ec888a16015d32d475ff33a43f72c950f81c4e85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" />. WLOG set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/d/17de501dcf4a988ddc6ab01d085d6a3ce42e0246.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y &gt;= 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/5/24573c829758428470c5e1e594821c9754766f4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y &lt;= 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" />. We need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/3/0c3a14339ee6a9d5cd1632e6d72693ddc0a35230.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2&gt;0.25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"113\" height=\"18\" />, and drawing the coordinate system with bounds <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/9/d892ffa23d5628cfd2e05b88eaf0c5d1657b084a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 0), (1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"184\" height=\"18\" /> gives probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/7/f7757c82195aea7d1b2adb12416965d1c6e4887d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1-\\frac{\\pi}{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"52\" height=\"33\" /> that the distance between the points is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/8/6d814fbc40ac21751a95cdbe9c0e39b272112f40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&gt;0.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Adding these up and finding the fraction gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/c/cbc11d714a70cc0a7487f7bb3387ff31dc20446d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{32} (26 - \\pi)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"86\" height=\"37\" /> for an answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/1/57161282d686773a30f443ce070c6a5057af16d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 59$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Average_Function_Value.2FQuick_Faux_Integration.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Average_Function_Value/Quick_Faux_Integration)\">Solution 3 (Average Function Value/Quick Faux Integration)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Average Function Value/Quick Faux Integration)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>WLOG, let the first point be on the bottom side of the square. The points where the second point could exist are outside a circle of radius 0.5 centered on the first point. The parts of the square that lie in this circle are the distance from the point to the closest side of the square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, the distance from the point to the outside of the circle (the radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" />), and any portion of the nearest side that lies within the circle as represented by the Pythagorean Theorem <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/b/8db5f49ac175ca9567a8abb5cb3747291e4ac3fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}-n^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"72\" height=\"43\" />. Thus, the total length the second point can exist in can be represented by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/9/aa9e99eb56cf0020b6d20dc75a92c9f47a879519.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(n)=4-(0.5+n+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}-n^2})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"257\" height=\"45\" />. Distributing, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/1/c6193ab81af889d6bd1e8aeec5cc253eae6ade67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(n)=3.5-n-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}-n^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"210\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p><p>Then, we can find the average of this function through calculus (wow more calc?). This formula is as follows,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/9/4a92c02b1d3ac40fc1c48ec0e63456b52c060d3d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{a_{f} -a_{i}} \\int_{a_{i}}^{a_{f}} f(x)\\;dx\\]\" width=\"156\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>For this case, the limits of integration are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/e/e3ea742e1444384e723e486b03b78b653f27f2c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\leq n\\leq 0.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"91\" height=\"15\" />). Then, we have,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/b/50be76240dd98c293f44cf39d84adb6b77cea348.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} 3.5-n-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}-n^2}\\;dn\\]\" width=\"218\" height=\"46\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/4/8e4b0d7e2b095f32545575d79bb287013f5a23c0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2(\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} 3.5\\;dn -\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}n\\;dn -\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}-n^2}\\;dn)\\]\" width=\"341\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Even if you don't know how to integrate, as long as you know the idea of integration, you can figure these out. Graphing the first two, you can see that the first is a rectangle of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> and width <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/b/87b8245e043508c7c928c89b42f9ce38f04d720d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3.5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" />. The second is an isosceles right triangle of leg length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/17429b0e94c6f0c1c4c75a70435d841fff304972.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} 3.5\\;dn=\\frac{7}{4}, \\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}n\\;dn=\\frac{1}{8}\\]\" width=\"228\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p>Recognize that the third integral is a semicircle of radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> and centered at the origin. This is where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/c/f2ca003a7da0de4994b4733e203b74ff52d42553.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pi$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> comes in. From <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" />, the integral is simply a quarter circle. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/d/27d81e154217c8b010ac3aa8e7c54f408e9aab1d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}^2\\pi}{4}=\\frac{\\pi}{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"78\" height=\"44\" />.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/f/84f57c2ab7e3914a5333bd60ffd1ef9cc0b76344.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}-n^2}\\;dn)=\\frac{\\pi}{16}\\]\" width=\"179\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p>If you want me to actually integrate these, look below. Do note that this is for those that have a limited knowledge of integration or those that have little time but are being very clever.\n</p><p>Overall, where second point could lie to satisfy the problem is a length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/d/56d989749d385ec6a2012caf3b70d33887dcffc3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2(\\frac{7}{4}-\\frac{\\pi}{16}-\\frac{1}{8})=\\frac{26-\\pi}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"193\" height=\"38\" />. By contrast, the total length where it could lie is the perimeter of the square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. So the possible points that the second point could be make up <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/2/f42ef286a85b466831f522b82a70c64b5a521677.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\frac{26-\\pi}{8}}{4}=\\frac{26-\\pi}{32}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"117\" height=\"41\" /> of the square's perimeter. Obviously, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/3/3c378df56438e9ff84d7c3b36b430b53535fc441.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(32, 26, 1)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"135\" height=\"18\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/d/5ddb5c53e25e257189192a17c1c30e9b8d76fc42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$32+26+1=59\\implies\\boxed{A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"207\" height=\"24\" />\n</p><p><i>Sorry for the long explanation!</i>\n</p>\n<pre><b>Actual Integration as Promised</b>\n</pre>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/4/8e4b0d7e2b095f32545575d79bb287013f5a23c0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2(\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} 3.5\\;dn -\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}n\\;dn -\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}-n^2}\\;dn)\\]\" width=\"341\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p>First two integrals easily done by power rule\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/d/4ad06aa8f9e1c1e5895353d6176466bc96b259fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} 3.5\\;dn=3.5\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} 1\\;dn=3.5n\\Big|_0^{\\frac{1}{2}}=7/4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"322\" height=\"46\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/d/d4d177063c30f2969bf644e4ba5c01e7082d65bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}n\\;dn= \\frac{n^2}{2}\\Big|_0^{\\frac{1}{2}}=1/8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"181\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p><br />\nLast integral by trig substitution (long)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/b/f7b42fe8057d8d8f82fa96b41767b30df4df3210.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}-n^2}\\;dn=\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\sqrt{1-(2n)^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"290\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/2/cd234249f45716821009314d86149e4ddcfc3218.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(u)=2n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/3/0c37459b3e36f7b7671f2c061dcfedb75fd9dda1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$dn=\\frac{\\cos(u)}{2}\\;du$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"122\" height=\"38\" /> (differentiate both sides). \n</p><p>Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/b/fab4feb63b9c2918d0575c1102396ed07ed8e74c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{n=0}^{n=\\frac{1}{4}}\\cos(u)\\sqrt{1-\\sin(u)^2}\\;du=\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{n=0}^{n=\\frac{1}{2}}\\cos(u)\\sqrt{\\cos^2(u)}\\;du=\\frac{1}{2}\\int_{n=0}^{n=\\frac{1}{2}}\\cos^2(u)\\;du$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"652\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p>This is a known integral that can be derived from further trig identities (specifically double angle). For the sake of brevity,  \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/6/c96a355ca353731cb41497a7b98d1c76f733d119.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{n=0}^{n=\\frac{1}{2}}\\cos^2(u)\\;du= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{2u + \\sin(2u)}{4})\\Big|_{x=0}^{x=\\frac{1}{2}}=\\frac{2u+\\sin(2u)}{16}\\Big|_{x=0}^{x=\\frac{1}{2}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"492\" height=\"46\" />.\n</p><p>Convert the limits: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/e/c6e7984dc55220b7634c0cc2649178e6377640ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(u)=2\\cdot0\\implies u_1=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"202\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/e/c9ef6eba979de5db2aba80d199a19b4e24f0de3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin(u)=2\\cdot\\frac{1}{2} \\implies u_2=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"211\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Finally, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/c/33c841bb55d5dd002fd4913374668a82fa88b134.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2u+\\sin(2u)}{16}\\Big|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}=\\frac{\\pi+\\sin(\\pi)}{16}-0=\\frac{\\pi}{16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"304\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p><br />\n~ Solution By BJHHar\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Extension.2C_CALCULUS.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Extension,_CALCULUS)\">Solution 4 (Extension, CALCULUS)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Extension, CALCULUS)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Set the problem up similarly to in solution 1, where we split the square into 8 segments. Notice that each segment is the same, so WLOG use any one of them. For the purposes of this solution, I will assume the segment we use starts at (0, 0) and ends at (0.5, 0). The square I use will have vertices of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/a/25a306093bbd9e0699c65dba033c9483cd379dc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/1/be11a56a55425d6c6b69f70f5fa98eab95e8ad18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/7/ae7a465ffe5255961382f5777569608be052b783.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />. \n</p><p>A way we can figure out when the distance is at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> is if we figure out when it isn't <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" />. Let's pick a point on our segment and denote it with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/9/eb99be0632ae9be9bbeeec99f75e4a71c2595180.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"18\" />. Then, there are three ways a second point can be within the boundaries of this first point. Either, it is to the left of it (if possible), to the right of it, or it is on the segment that forms a right angle with it. \n</p><p>Obviously, there is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> distance if the point is to the left of the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. Since we denoted this segment to be from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" />, then there will always be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> distance to the right of the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> (as that's the maximum that we are trying to figure out).\n</p><p>The difficult part is finding the total length on the segment that is perpendicular to our segment. However, since the square has a right angle, we can first find that the segment should have a length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/5/0c5410c6531b1442158bf15212218daea187b028.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{0.25-x^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" /> (by the Pythagorean theorem, with hypotenuse 0.5 and one leg being x).\n</p><p>Now that we have our three distances, all we need to do is find the average value of them. We can best do this with \"Integral/Interval\", and so we take the integral from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/c/bccb223b3be3fcbddae0f1ec04fee49c4bac05b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+0.5+\\sqrt{0.25-x^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"165\" height=\"18\" /> and then divide it by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> (the interval). To integrate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/5/0c5410c6531b1442158bf15212218daea187b028.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{0.25-x^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" /> by hand, we want to pull out the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/8/8f88d403085fe6600059002646348a5f267266d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" />, and then apply u-sub and our integration rules to find the answer. \n</p><p>We get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/6/0a64dfc93ca057db56ef8b65b5bfcd3a0e4d316e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.75 - \\frac{\\pi}{16} \\cdot 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"98\" height=\"33\" />. Simplify this into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/3/503ed2e59e98ba7dbde949904f6560e97ac04214.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6-\\pi}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"44\" height=\"37\" />. \n</p><p>Now we are on our last stage. Proceed to make this equivalent to what the question is asking, as we have found the probability that the second point is within a distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" />, whereas the question asks for at least a distance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/2/192b4a258ee998961a99523fd0f0a029f9ea194a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0.5$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> (so more than). We can do this simply by doing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/0/58001a4c774467e9d3c01c89ef7b7a2bf2608263.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4- \\frac{6-\\pi}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"76\" height=\"37\" /> (as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the total amount of length). This equates to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/c/abce4b50801eb45e947e7719f438cba5ef24867d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{26-\\pi}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"57\" height=\"38\" /> and then we divide by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> as that is the total amount of length (remember this is probability). \n</p><p>Thus, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/c/d4ca7c53993c71a77748785903cbd3a109dbe22a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{26-\\pi}{32}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"53\" height=\"37\" /> for our probability, and so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/6/4867ea46930cbc07cd1b205afec5ac90e7388fdc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$26 + 1 + 32 = \\textbf{(A)}\\ 59$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"166\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>Some notes: I tried to explain everything but it's quite difficult to explain - there is a way of non-calculus (like there always is) that I think was mentioned above, something with circles (since the thing under the square root is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/9/07943e4018804fb52d4f6f9cfff639ff00f54511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2 = \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"93\" height=\"37\" />, so finding the average value of that isn't difficult). \n</p><p>IronicNinja~ Edited by AngelaLZ~\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Area.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Area)\">Solution 5 (Area)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Area)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Choose a certain side for one of the points to be on. Let the distance from the point to the vertex on its left be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />\n</p><p>We split this into two cases: \n</p><p>Case 1: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/3/413a8dc56a49c61c56ce8d06a5a65a668c2b9774.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\leq x\\leq \\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"79\" height=\"37\" />:\n</p><p>The total length of the segments for which the other point can be on such that the straight-line distance between the points is less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc960c165b21ba43f7a90caa2893d40409d6944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/4/9e4d9145f823a901b4fd02b506974a58b757a3ad.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{\\frac14-x^2}+x+\\frac12.\\]\" width=\"141\" height=\"43\" /> We can graph this in the Cartesian plane and find the area of the region below the curve and above the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/8/ec89f09c84538ec43930ca5d8e5e0957e2000822.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>Case 2: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/a/17aa483af6fcdf22add43bc1243ae322f7c8e9a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12&lt; x\\leq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"80\" height=\"37\" />:\n</p><p>This is basically Case 1 but flipped over the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/5/da5cdb297400b28196aeb8ea617b007db7dc9c23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"46\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>So our total probability is 1 minus the area of the graph over the total area (4, perimeter of square). Notice that the desired area of the region below the curve we found earlier is the sum of a quarter circle with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc960c165b21ba43f7a90caa2893d40409d6944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> and centered at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> and a trapezoid with height <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc960c165b21ba43f7a90caa2893d40409d6944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> and bases of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc960c165b21ba43f7a90caa2893d40409d6944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/d/add685ffcf9823b32ee3100dc9e8a85549e223f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac32$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. Adding this all up then multiplying by 2, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/e/70edea81c4dc90b726958e93e87f341c9ac3861b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{\\pi}{8}+\\frac34\\]\" width=\"46\" height=\"38\" /> and then the probability of the desired result would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/f/ebfbf50dc850e07a41682b7d735e851070e8226b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1-\\frac{\\pi+6}{32}=\\frac{26-\\pi}{32}\\]\" width=\"153\" height=\"37\" /> and our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/0/eb0305c70a807d54a8008233a8003668abc665b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$26+1+32=\\boxed{59}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"144\" height=\"23\" />. ~caroline2023\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Richard_Rusczyk\">Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/videos/amc/2015amc12a/399\">https://artofproblemsolving.com/videos/amc/2015amc12a/399</a>\n</p><p>~ dolphin7\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A\" title=\"2015 AMC 12A\">2015 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2015 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2015 AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2015 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 22\">Problem 22</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"2015 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 24\">Problem 24</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_1\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2015 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2015 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php